So because they hated
their Communist enemies,

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:16.050
Taiwan rejected the Romanized
alphabet that Mao was pushing

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:18.360
and stuck with this traditional alphabet

00:12:18.360 --> 00:12:21.010
that had been developed
a few years earlier.

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:22.861
And that's still how it is today.

00:12:22.861 --> 00:12:26.278
(woman speaking Chinese)

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.130
So this gets to a pretty
satisfying answer to the question

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:32.210
of this entire video

00:12:32.210 --> 00:12:35.990
which is how do Chinese speakers
type on a qwerty keyboard.

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:37.780
The answer is, if you go to mainland China

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.850
you're going to see keyboards like this.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:44.320
People in mainland China use
this keyboard to type out

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:48.270
the sounds of their words,
and the computer takes that

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:51.140
and renders it into Chinese characters.

00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:53.440
If you live in Taiwan,
you'll see a keyboard

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:55.040
that looks more like this,

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.660
but you basically do the same thing.

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:00.540
Use these characters to type
out the sounds of the word

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:03.600
and the computer will render
it into a Chinese character.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:06.240
Both of these are new writing systems

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.430
that were developed in the past 100 years

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:11.996
to help Chinese speakers
spell out their words

00:13:11.996 --> 00:13:15.249
and move away from a
character-based system.

00:13:15.249 --> 00:13:16.810
- [Narrator] Now what are the key features

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:18.570
that you think should
be in an ideal laptop.

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:20.099
- [Woman] Standby for the
software transmission.

00:13:20.099 --> 00:13:20.932
Better start your recorder now.

00:13:20.932 --> 00:13:22.820
- Okay, back to our timeline here.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:26.900
It's the '80s and '90s. China
is starting to really adopt

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.227
technology and they're
using these typing methods

00:13:29.227 --> 00:13:32.768
to use the qwerty keyboards
to actually participate

00:13:32.768 --> 00:13:34.220
in the computing world.

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.700
But they're still lagging so
far behind the Western world.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.690
They're way slower in their
typing so the Chinese government

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.440
went back to the drawing
boards and was like,

00:13:42.440 --> 00:13:44.310
how can we make it faster.

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.990
And boy, they found a solution in the '80s

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:48.665
that will change everything.

00:13:48.665 --> 00:13:52.720
(woman speaking Chinese)

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:54.990
And this is the work that's
kind of juicy, in my opinion.

00:13:54.990 --> 00:13:57.480
This is the part that
is like, I don't know,

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.900
really helps me to understand
how people type today.

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:02.240
You know when you go to Google

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.630
and you start typing a sentence,

00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:07.300
and it fills out the rest
of the sentence for you,

00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:10.810
or even on Gmail these
days, like I'll be typing

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:14.460
and suddenly it'll like
predict what I want to say.

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:16.830
It's pretty cool. It's not life-changing.

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:18.500
It's sort of like saves
me maybe a couple of

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:19.900
milliseconds every day.

00:14:19.900 --> 00:14:23.900
In China, this technology
of predictive text,

00:14:23.900 --> 00:14:27.050
predictive typing, was life-changing

00:14:27.050 --> 00:14:29.150
because regardless of what method you use

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:32.480
to start building your
characters in the computer,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:34.184
the computer now starts to guess

00:14:34.184 --> 00:14:36.570
what characters you want to type.

00:14:36.570 --> 00:14:39.610
For example, let's say someone
is using one of these systems

00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:40.970
that we're gonna use to
build these characters,

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:43.210
and they type this character.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:47.530
This is the root of a Chinese
word and it means "water".

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.200
The computer sees this and says,

00:14:50.200 --> 00:14:51.710
okay, you just typed "water".

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:53.700
There's a bunch of
characters that are derived

00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:56.320
from this root, this "water" root.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.200
For example, here's the one for "river"

00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:00.700
or maybe you actually want this one

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.030
which is the word for "wash".

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:07.250
So if you just type water,
you can look at these options

00:15:07.250 --> 00:15:09.370
and decide which one you want.

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:12.278
This was an algorithmic,
predictive typing system

00:15:12.278 --> 00:15:16.330
that was happening in
the late 1980s in China,

00:15:16.330 --> 00:15:18.610
three whole decades
before anything similar

00:15:18.610 --> 00:15:19.740
surfaced in the West.

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.350
And it is this technology
of predictive text

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.960
that changed everything for China.

00:15:24.960 --> 00:15:26.960
- [Narrator] Before computers,
there was no practical way

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:30.010
to type the thousands of
characters called kanji.

00:15:30.010 --> 00:15:32.430
To deal with the complexity
of these symbol words

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:35.210
different systems have been adopted.

00:15:35.210 --> 00:15:38.020
- They started to refine
this algorithm to make it

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.360
more and more clear that
as soon as you start

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:42.431
typing anything, the algorithm says,

00:15:42.431 --> 00:15:45.710
do you want to do this,
or this, or this, or this?

00:15:45.710 --> 00:15:50.220
I mean I've been watching a
great deal of competitions,

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.440
typing competitions.

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:54.857
(woman speaking Chinese)

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:57.860
This is the thing in China,

00:15:57.860 --> 00:16:01.210
and if you zoom in and look
closely at this competition,

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:04.680
you'll see that as soon as
these competitors start typing

00:16:04.680 --> 00:16:06.286
a pop-up box comes up, giving them

00:16:06.286 --> 00:16:09.820
an algorithmically
generated menu of options

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.290
that predicts what they're trying to type.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:16.460
So typing in Chinese is as
much about choosing from

00:16:16.460 --> 00:16:19.390
this predictive menu as it
is about pressing the keys.

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:20.840
It is a combination of both.

00:16:20.840 --> 00:16:23.470
And this competition has
pushed developers to make

00:16:23.470 --> 00:16:25.810
better and better predictive algorithms

00:16:25.810 --> 00:16:28.450
so that people can
communicate very efficiently

00:16:28.450 --> 00:16:29.640
using the keyboard.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:31.930
So how fast are these
people typing right now?

00:16:31.930 --> 00:16:34.195
Well, the average English
speaker can type at

00:16:34.195 --> 00:16:36.890
around 43 words per minute.

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:38.820
I work on a computer all day
so I'm probably more like

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:41.010
60, 65 words per minute.

00:16:41.010 --> 00:16:44.260
There is an English typing
competition in Las Vegas

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:49.260
and the typists here are
mind-blowing 163 words per minute.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:53.890
But if you head back to these
guys, these people are typing

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:58.323
at a score of 242 words per minute,

00:16:59.450 --> 00:17:01.283
four times faster than I can type,

00:17:02.270 --> 00:17:04.610
and almost double what
they're typing in English

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:07.283
at the best competition in Las Vegas.

00:17:08.834 --> 00:17:10.030
Whoa!

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:10.874
- Wow, that's fast.

00:17:10.874 --> 00:17:11.740
- Yeah, it's fast.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:13.620
- So China used this technology

00:17:13.620 --> 00:17:15.600
that was developed in the West

00:17:15.600 --> 00:17:17.540
mainly designed for languages

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:20.040
that are very different than Chinese,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.454
and they mastered it, but guess what?

00:17:22.454 --> 00:17:26.950
The Chinese government still
has the need for speed.

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:29.890
They're like, how can we make it faster?

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:32.888
So predictive typing in China
went from letting you start

00:17:32.888 --> 00:17:35.320
a character and predicting

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.040
what character you want to choose,

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:40.310
to then going to seeing
what character you just put

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:41.550
and predicting the next one,

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:44.140
the next word in the sentence
that you want to say.

00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:45.510
And now in the last few years

00:17:45.510 --> 00:17:47.150
they're pushing it to the next level,

00:17:47.150 --> 00:17:49.790
which is Cloud-based predictive texting.

00:17:49.790 --> 00:17:51.660
- A cloud?

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:53.770
- Again, the closest thing
we have in the West is like

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:55.090
the Google Auto-complete.

00:17:55.090 --> 00:17:56.100
You go to this search engine.

00:17:56.100 --> 00:17:58.890
You start typing and the Internet is like,

00:17:58.890 --> 00:18:00.220
a lot of people are searching this

00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:02.290
so we think you're searching this, too.

00:18:02.290 --> 00:18:04.642
But imagine, instead of
just a search engine,

00:18:04.642 --> 00:18:07.680
imagine this concept for everywhere.

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:09.920
As you're typing your WORD documents,

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:12.470
as you're texting your mom,

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:15.120
anywhere in the digital
space that is connected

00:18:15.120 --> 00:18:18.620
to the Internet is now
feeding you sentences

00:18:18.620 --> 00:18:20.870
that they think maybe you want to say

00:18:20.870 --> 00:18:25.140
based on an AI-generated
web of information of

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:29.270
what everyone else is texting,
and emailing and writing.

00:18:29.270 --> 00:18:30.610
So an example of this,

00:18:30.610 --> 00:18:32.420
let's say that there's
a Chinese movie star

00:18:32.420 --> 00:18:34.800
that just got into a big car accident.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:37.180
It's all over the news and
everybody is talking about it.

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.780
You've never typed this
movie star's name before

00:18:39.780 --> 00:18:41.820
but as soon as you do start typing it

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:44.890
your phone starts talking to the Cloud

00:18:44.890 --> 00:18:47.790
and not just the Internet but
other people's text messages.

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:49.580
So then the algorithm says that I think

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:53.020
you probably want to text
about this movie star

00:18:53.020 --> 00:18:54.180
and the car accident.

00:18:54.180 --> 00:18:57.600
And this seems like a great
time-saving technology

00:18:57.600 --> 00:18:58.830
that helps Chinese speakers type.

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.270
- It's a good idea.

00:19:00.270 --> 00:19:03.330
- But just imagine some scenarios here.

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:05.390
Let's say that you're not writing about

00:19:05.390 --> 00:19:07.400
a movie star and the car accident.

00:19:07.400 --> 00:19:10.170
But let's say instead you
are texting a friend about

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:14.990
June 4th 1989, Tiananmen Square.

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:16.470
- [Narrator] The noise of gunfire rose

00:19:16.470 --> 00:19:18.440
from all over the center of Peking.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.950
- [Narrator] China is a
nation at war with itself.

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:24.860
- There's one major company
that owns the software.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.632
This one company determines most of

00:19:27.632 --> 00:19:30.350
what shows up in predictive text.

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:33.641
And in a country where there's
not a lot of hesitation

00:19:33.641 --> 00:19:37.040
to control citizens'
access to information,

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:40.160
it's not far-stretched to
see how subtle manipulation

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:43.164
can start to occur with
this Cloud-predictive text.

00:19:43.164 --> 00:19:46.515
Algorithm deciding sort
of what should go next.

00:19:46.515 --> 00:19:50.980
For example, if you type
in "Taiwan is China",

00:19:50.980 --> 00:19:53.407
the algorithm will suggest to you

00:19:53.407 --> 00:19:57.670
"Taiwan is an inseparable part of China".

00:19:57.670 --> 00:19:59.990
Anyway, this is not a
major problem right now.

00:19:59.990 --> 00:20:03.140
And I'm not saying that the
government is manipulating

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.980
every individual in China
with predictive text.

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:09.770
All I'm saying is that, the
story of how Chinese speakers

00:20:09.770 --> 00:20:12.523
have been able to type
on the qwerty keyboard

00:20:12.523 --> 00:20:15.980
is the story of really clever and amazing

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:17.500
technological advancements,

00:20:17.500 --> 00:20:21.580
and a huge part of that has
been predictive algorithms.

00:20:21.580 --> 00:20:24.360
And all I'm saying is that
algorithmically presented

00:20:24.360 --> 00:20:28.040
